{
  "summary": "Flushes buffered block and undo data explicitly before the writer goes out of scope, catching write exceptions, logging them, and triggering a fatal node shutdown. In `BufferedWriter::flush()`, clears pending bytes before writing to prevent the destructor from re-attempting a failed write during exception unwinding. Adds an `Assume` in the destructor to ensure callers flush explicitly.",
  "problem": "When disk writes fail (such as on a full filesystem), `AutoFile::write_buffer()` throws an exception. Because `BufferedWriter`'s destructor is implicitly non-throwing, an unhandled flush exception in the destructor invokes `std::terminate` instead of shutting down gracefully. Furthermore, failed flushes left pending bytes in the buffer, causing the destructor to throw a second time while unwinding.",
  "evidence": [
    "Author description: \"fwrite() can report fewer bytes written than requested after a local storage error, e.g., if the filesystem fills up while a block is being written (my 1 TB benchmarking servers have recently been filling up, so I've been seeing these failures more often).\"",
    "PR description identifies the bug as a regression introduced in #31551."
  ],
